WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:03.179
If you’re a gamer, you probably use Discord
to chat with friends.

00:00:03.179 --> 00:00:08.080
Heck, even if you’re not a gamer, you probably
still use Discord these days.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:12.990
Everyone uses Discord, but no one seems to
know exactly why Discord is completely destroying

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:14.180
all the other chat apps.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:18.320
And that’s a shame, because the founder
has one of the most interesting stories I’ve

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:19.830
ever heard in Silicon Valley.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:23.930
His name is Jason Citron, and he just became
a billionaire with Discord’s recent massive

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:27.174
fundraise at a $7 billion dollar valuation,
but he didn’t go to an Ivy League school,

00:00:27.174 --> 00:00:30.490
and he never worked at a big tech company.

00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:35.410
His path to success broke so many Silicon
Valley conventions, and every one of his strategies

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:38.590
can be directly copied if you’re building
a startup yourself.

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:41.110
Like most successful founders, Jason is super
humble.

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:46.310
In fact, when he is asked about why Discord
became successful, he usually just says something

00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:47.440
simple like this:

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:52.789
“We were successful because our product
was ten times better than and ten times easier

00:00:52.789 --> 00:00:55.629
to use than the things that came before it,
so people have been switching”

00:00:55.629 --> 00:00:59.680
And sure, building a great product is key
to every startup success, but that doesn’t

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:02.699
really explain how Discord became so popular
in the beginning.

00:01:02.699 --> 00:01:06.280
What does Jason know about growing a startup
that the rest of us don’t?

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:09.710
In many ways, Discord was late to the party
in communication software.

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:14.600
They have plenty of competitors in the gaming
space, and Slack offers similar functionality

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:15.600
to businesses.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.369
Slack actually launched two years before Discord
and now that SalesForce acquired them for

00:01:20.369 --> 00:01:25.359
$27 billion dollars, they should basically
have limitless resources to compete with Discord,

00:01:25.359 --> 00:01:27.459
but Slack seems to be falling behind.

00:01:27.459 --> 00:01:31.810
Which raises the question: how did Discord
get so big, even though they launched two

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:33.569
years later than Slack?

00:01:33.569 --> 00:01:38.049
Most social apps fail completely, but when
they succeed and get big, they tend to get

00:01:38.049 --> 00:01:41.240
really big, and I think Discord is just getting
started.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:45.819
The company recently passed 140 million users
and has completely broken out of the gaming

00:01:45.819 --> 00:01:47.359
niche where they got started.

00:01:47.359 --> 00:01:50.969
That type of growth is extremely hard to come
by in Silicon Valley and it really shows that

00:01:50.969 --> 00:01:54.969
Jason has a unique strategy that differs from
other startup founders.

00:01:54.969 --> 00:01:58.990
Fortunately, the way Jason thinks about growing
a startup can be summarized in just three

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:00.240
key points.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:03.829
So let’s dig in to try and understand the
mind of Jason Citron.

00:02:03.829 --> 00:02:08.450
Jason’s biggest strength is actually something
that many people might think of as a weakness.

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:12.849
He didn’t have any impressive qualifications
before becoming an entrepreneur.

00:02:12.849 --> 00:02:16.851
Instead of studying engineering at MIT or
getting a business degree from Harvard, he

00:02:16.851 --> 00:02:20.980
attended a small school in Florida named Full
Sail University.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:25.010
The school specializes in media and arts,
and isn’t exactly known for churning out

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:29.480
successful startup founders, but Jason knew
he was interested in video games, and Full

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:32.730
Sail University has a great program for game-design.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:37.030
Jason had already taught himself to program
at a young age, so when he got to college,

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:41.600
he was able to focus on applying those skills
to something he loved, making video games.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:46.120
After college, he went to work for a series
of game studios making iPhone apps and actually

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:48.310
won some awards for mobile game design.

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:52.830
After only two years working for other people,
Jason quickly struck out on his own to start

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:53.830
a company.

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:56.980
Even though he didn’t have an impressive
resume, Jason knew that he could build something

00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:01.019
gamers would enjoy, and that’s Jason’s
first key advantage as an entrepreneur: he’s

00:03:01.019 --> 00:03:02.890
a relentless builder.

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:05.939
People like to think that in order to start
a massive company, you either have to drop

00:03:05.939 --> 00:03:10.210
out of college at a young age, or graduate
from a top school, but that’s simply not

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:11.210
the case.

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:13.400
Let’s look at some data to see what I’m
talking about here.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:18.420
This data set was compiled from over a hundred
unicorn companies, meaning they have been

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:20.560
valued at over a billion dollars.

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:25.469
More than 35% of these unicorns were founded
by people with only a bachelor’s degree.

00:03:25.469 --> 00:03:28.989
And more founders had PhDs than were dropouts.

00:03:28.989 --> 00:03:32.719
So the myth of the magical dropout founder
is just that, a myth.

00:03:32.719 --> 00:03:37.380
Clearly, no matter what your educational background
is, you can start a great company if you solve

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:41.109
the right problem and create a great product,
and Jason clearly knows that.

00:03:41.109 --> 00:03:45.629
And when it comes to attending prestigious
schools, it might surprise you to hear that

00:03:45.629 --> 00:03:50.599
just as many founders of billion-dollar companies
graduated from schools ranked in the top ten

00:03:50.599 --> 00:03:54.269
as from schools with rankings that didn’t
even crack the top one hundred.

00:03:54.269 --> 00:03:58.529
That’s the amazing thing about entrepreneurship:
it doesn’t require anyone’s permission.

00:03:58.529 --> 00:04:03.620
Getting a job at a top tech company or working
at a fancy investment bank is incredibly difficult

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:07.379
if you don’t have the right school on your
resume, but build something people want, and

00:04:07.379 --> 00:04:10.269
investors will look past your background in
a heartbeat.

00:04:10.269 --> 00:04:14.079
Having the right credentials didn’t matter
to Jason, he just wanted to make great games,

00:04:14.079 --> 00:04:15.849
so he started building.

00:04:15.849 --> 00:04:20.319
In 2008, he launched Aurora Feint, which was
a puzzle game for iPhones that had a lot of

00:04:20.319 --> 00:04:22.420
really compelling role playing elements.

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:26.639
The game was fairly successful, but ultimately
couldn’t gain mass adoption because of its

00:04:26.639 --> 00:04:28.110
high price point.

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:33.569
Most games charged just one dollar, so it
was hard to get attention if you charged more.

00:04:33.569 --> 00:04:37.889
Even worse for Jason, some developers had
already figured out how to sneakily offer

00:04:37.889 --> 00:04:41.260
in-app purchases, even though they weren’t
technically allowed by Apple.

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:46.220
Jason had made a very common mistake by focusing
too much on the core product and leaving the

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:47.849
business model as an afterthought.

00:04:47.849 --> 00:04:51.540
Although it’s incredibly important to build
something great, you can’t put off thinking

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:52.840
about how you’ll make money forever.

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:57.190
Besides, a gaming company that goes out of
business isn’t going to make any gamers

00:04:57.190 --> 00:05:00.919
happy at all, so it’s important to test
your business model a bit before you commit

00:05:00.919 --> 00:05:01.919
to it fully.

00:05:01.919 --> 00:05:05.979
With Aurora Feint struggling to make money,
and the company running out of cash, Jason

00:05:05.979 --> 00:05:07.180
decided to pivot.

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:11.259
The games were underperforming, but the company
had built some really solid technology.

00:05:11.259 --> 00:05:16.330
This was before Apple had announced Game Center,
and it was still a huge hassle for game developers

00:05:16.330 --> 00:05:18.639
to add social features to their games.

00:05:18.639 --> 00:05:22.730
Having the ability to invite friends to play
games with you could easily make a game go

00:05:22.730 --> 00:05:23.730
viral.

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:27.479
That’s what happened with Words with Friends,
which was a social version of Scrabble for

00:05:27.479 --> 00:05:31.770
mobile devices and Draw Something, which applied
the same principles to Pictionary.

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:36.990
Both apps grew organically because every user
would invite their friends to play with them

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:39.220
and create a positive feedback loop.

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:42.920
Playing games with real friends online is
way more fun than playing against a computer

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:48.020
or a random person, so social features quickly
became a must have for every mobile game.

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:52.500
But building these social features was often
difficult and game developers were more interested

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:54.940
in improving their core game mechanics.

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:59.030
Connecting to the user’s address book and
linking up Facebook accounts was a very boring

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:03.340
programming task that no developer wanted
to get stuck with, so those features often

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:06.200
got left to the last minute, or put off entirely.

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:11.539
Jason’s idea was to offer these social features
as a neatly packaged software development

00:06:11.539 --> 00:06:14.539
kit that would make adding these features
quick and easy.

00:06:14.539 --> 00:06:15.949
There was only one problem though.

00:06:15.949 --> 00:06:19.520
His team hadn’t actually built any of that
and they didn’t have the time to.

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:23.270
They were only 3 weeks away from bankruptcy
and couldn’t afford to spend all that time

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:26.889
building this new idea unless they were absolutely
sure it would work.

00:06:26.889 --> 00:06:31.250
But that’s where Jason’s second key advantage
comes in: He’s a master of prototyping.

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:35.310
Even though Jason didn’t have time to build
a full-fledged product, he could still keep

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:39.650
the company in business if he proved that
people wanted this new social platform.

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:44.410
So he set up a landing page for the product
which he called “OpenFeint” and outlined

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:47.509
exactly what the product could do for game
developers.

00:06:47.509 --> 00:06:51.070
Nothing had actually been built yet, but people
visiting the page didn’t know that, they

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:55.729
just saw a new tool that could rid them of
a tedious task, let them launch faster, and

00:06:55.729 --> 00:06:57.870
ideally make their games go viral.

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:01.960
In order to drive traffic to the new site,
Jason pitched the story to TechCrunch and

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:05.130
convinced a reporter to write an explainer
article about OpenFeint.

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:10.120
Overnight, they got 400 developers to sign
up, which completely validated the concept.

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:14.409
Jason and his team worked hard to build OpenFeint
into a real product and get it in the hands

00:07:14.409 --> 00:07:15.409
of game developers.

00:07:15.409 --> 00:07:19.270
When you’re working on a new startup idea,
it’s extremely common to get sucked into

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:23.340
developing the actual product, especially
if you’re a software engineer and enjoy

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:24.340
coding.

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:25.919
I’ve certainly been in that situation myself.

00:07:25.919 --> 00:07:30.550
What makes Jason so unique as an entrepreneur
is that he can balance his programming skills

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:32.960
with prototyping to validate his ideas.

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:37.060
Obviously the cash crunch motivated his decision
to launch the simple landing page.

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:38.470
It was really his only option.

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:42.889
But validating your startup idea before you
start building is always an option.

00:07:42.889 --> 00:07:45.219
So be sure to use prototyping effectively
like Jason.

00:07:45.219 --> 00:07:49.600
The strategy worked out perfectly and OpenFeint
grew significantly.

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:54.770
The growth was so strong that just two years
after launching, a Japanese company bought

00:07:54.770 --> 00:07:57.520
OpenFeint for over $100 million dollars.

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:01.639
This was an amazing windfall for a guy who
was just 26 years old and didn’t come from

00:08:01.639 --> 00:08:05.129
a wealthy family, but it wouldn’t all be
smooth sailing from there.

00:08:05.129 --> 00:08:08.810
Even though he had sold the company, Jason
originally planned to stay onboard at the

00:08:08.810 --> 00:08:11.750
company and help build an even better version
of OpenFeint.

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:15.500
But the new management didn’t see eye-to-eye
with him, and so they asked him to resign.

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:18.159
Fs in the chat for an ousted founder.

00:08:18.159 --> 00:08:19.159
You hate to see it.

00:08:19.159 --> 00:08:21.860
But leaving OpenFeint behind was definitely
a good thing in hindsight.

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:26.449
It let Jason get back to what he loved doing
most, building great software for gamers.

00:08:26.449 --> 00:08:28.580
So he set out to make a new game.

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:34.090
This time, he built a multiplayer online battle
arena for iPads called Hammer &amp; Chisel.

00:08:34.090 --> 00:08:38.260
The basic idea was to bring an experience
like DOTA2 or League of Legends to mobile

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:40.760
devices and the initial response was quite
good.

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:45.650
He pitched the game at TechCrunch disrupt
in 2014 and received lots of positive feedback,

00:08:45.650 --> 00:08:51.200
but true virality just wasn’t there, and
Hammer &amp; Chisel fell short of breakout success,

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:53.370
much like AuroraFeint had 6 years earlier.

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:58.400
So once again, Jason refocused on finding
a problem that all gamers struggled with,

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:02.530
and just like with OpenFeint, he landed on
connecting socially while gaming.

00:09:02.530 --> 00:09:06.240
Even though he had been focused on mobile
up until this point, Jason and his team realized

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:09.200
that the PC would be a much easier platform
to target.

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:12.850
There were millions of PC gamers who were
open to trying new software, and building

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:17.501
a communication app for desktop PCs would
be easier than building something that would

00:09:17.501 --> 00:09:19.380
run smoothly on a mobile device while gaming.

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:23.160
So he started building the first version of
Discord, with the goal of bringing great chat

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:25.070
and voice communication to gamers.

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:29.900
Discord was an almost immediate success, and
lots of people credit the app's improved design

00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:34.070
over competitors as the main reason for Discord’s
growth, but I think there’s something more

00:09:34.070 --> 00:09:35.070
going on here.

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:39.540
First off, Discord didn’t just look different
from competitors like TeamSpeak or Mumble.

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:43.480
Although, we do need to take a second and
just look at how old those apps looked at

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:44.930
the time that Discord launched.

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:45.930
Ridiculous.

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:50.430
The more important design innovation here
was the fact that Discord could run in a browser,

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:52.560
so on-boarding was extremely easy.

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:56.620
As long as there was one person in a group
of gamers willing to quickly set up a Discord

00:09:56.620 --> 00:09:59.590
server, everyone else could just be invited
with a simple link.

00:09:59.590 --> 00:10:03.300
They wouldn’t need to install any program
to get up and running, something all the other

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:04.340
chat apps required.

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:08.430
But that wasn’t the real reason people stuck
around and switched to Discord permanently.

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:11.600
See, Discord is all about building authentic
communities.

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:16.120
A Discord server might start with a few friends,
or a specific topic in mind, but what really

00:10:16.120 --> 00:10:20.270
makes people keep opening the Discord app
is the feeling of a sense of community.

00:10:20.270 --> 00:10:24.410
And that’s Jason’s third key advantage:
he is a great community builder.

00:10:24.410 --> 00:10:28.880
This comes from the fact that Jason himself
is both a game developer and a gamer.

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:33.130
He understands the impact that clear and reliable
voice communication can have while playing

00:10:33.130 --> 00:10:34.130
a competitive game.

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:38.470
He’s an authentic member of the gaming community
and you can feel that every time you open

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:39.470
Discord.

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:40.480
But it doesn’t stop there.

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:45.400
When Jason initially launched Discord, he
created a dedicated server just to help new

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:46.400
users.

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:50.380
His entire team would be there to answer any
questions users had and troubleshoot any issues

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:51.380
that came up.

00:10:51.380 --> 00:10:55.500
He was also able to seed Discord into the
right communities by having friends post on

00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:58.830
specific subreddits that were dedicated to
particular games.

00:10:58.830 --> 00:11:03.690
The Final Fantasy Fourteen subreddit was particularly
critical for Discord’s growth in the early

00:11:03.690 --> 00:11:08.070
stage, since these players needed reliable
voice chat to communicate with their friends

00:11:08.070 --> 00:11:09.070
in game.

00:11:09.070 --> 00:11:13.690
By building a strong community of early adopters,
and making Discord easy to share with friends,

00:11:13.690 --> 00:11:18.080
Jason had finally created the viral product
he never could as a game developer.

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:22.230
Discord has been growing exponentially since
launch, and I personally hope it only gets

00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:23.230
more popular.

00:11:23.230 --> 00:11:27.890
Jason Citron is clearly a super talented founder
and someone we should all try and emulate

00:11:27.890 --> 00:11:30.120
while building the next generation of startups.

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:33.940
If you want to chat about entrepreneurship,
you can always DM me on Twitter, and please

00:11:33.940 --> 00:11:37.810
check out this recommended video, the YouTube
algorithm thinks you’ll really like it.

